From 9dbdc27abb67e52e8a26e452f265c3174e73bb8c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gili Tzabari <cowwoc2020@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 05:57:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Use LLVM 23 for strict Windows Rust links

Select hermetic LLVM 0.8.24 and align the direct Bazel dependencies selected
by its graph. Rebase the custom libc++, ARM64 MinGW runtime, and native
warning features to its toolchain layout. Keep MSVC compile and linker
warnings fatal using their driver-specific options.

Include focused Windows GNU -nolibc and MSVC linker-warning regressions.
The latter checks both the warning-only control and fatal-warning case.

Base: rust-v0.162.1 (092d3acd6bec3e3a14bdc7e7a2810ab628ab759d), with the
preceding release patch queue applied.
Red: bazel test //tools/build-warnings/llvm-driver:windows_nolibc_driver_test
with LLVM 22.1.8 exits 1 for unused -nolibc under -Werror.
Green: bazel test //tools/build-warnings/llvm-driver:all with LLVM 23.1.2
exits 0; both included regressions pass.
Maintained just bazel-lock-update and just bazel-lock-check exit 0.
Focused evidence: .cat/work/temp/llvm-toolchain-Rkha0k/
Follow-up red: the emitted LLVM 23 GNU Make bootstrap initially fails
configuration because the combined link resource directory lacks stddef.h.
Expand the compiler DirectoryInfo into files before resource filtering.
The bootstrap then exposes a GNU Make counter read only by assert; compile
that counter only when assertions are enabled. The same maintained Bazel
Make bootstrap target now builds and executes successfully without warnings.

Full native, emitted-patch, release-platform, and hosted cache acceptance
remain separate required gates.

Refs: https://github.com/holdmyspot-com/codex-unleashed/issues/60
Refs: https://github.com/holdmyspot-com/codex-unleashed/issues/61
---
